weight-in-lift-answer-why = Weight is the force with which a body pulls on its hanger. While the lift stands or moves steadily, the spring only holds the weights: P = m·g, up or down alike. For the weights to speed up upwards with the cabin, the spring must pull them harder than the Earth does — it stretches more: the weight grows. That happens when the lift starts up and when it slows down on the way down — both times the acceleration points up. When the acceleration points down (starting down, slowing at the top), the spring pulls weaker — the weight is less. What matters is not which way the lift goes, but which way it accelerates. And if the weights fall together with the dynamometer, the spring has nothing to hold: the weight is zero — weightlessness. The weights’ mass stays the same all the time, and so does the force of gravity. How to count the weight with the acceleration, P = m·(g + a), comes in grade 9.
